# /remotion — English in, rendered video out

[Remotion](https://www.remotion.dev) renders React components frame by frame
into real video files. Every run delivers a **pair**, never just a file:

| Artifact | What it's for |
|---|---|
| `<projdir>/` | Remotion project (TSX source). The source of truth. Edit it, re-render, keep it in git |
| `<projdir>/out/<slug>.mp4` | the rendered video (or `.webm` / `.gif` if asked) |

## Step 0 — Preflight

```bash
command -v node >/dev/null && node --version || echo "NODE_MISSING"
command -v npx  >/dev/null || echo "NPX_MISSING"
command -v ffprobe >/dev/null && echo "FFPROBE_OK" || echo "FFPROBE_NONE (optional, Remotion bundles its own ffmpeg)"
```

If `NODE_MISSING` or `NPX_MISSING`: stop. Remotion needs Node.js 18+ (Bun can
run the CLI but Node is the supported path). Tell the user to install Node
and re-run. Do not try to install Node yourself.

**License note (say it once per session, one line):** Remotion is free for
individuals and companies with up to 3 employees; larger companies need a
Remotion company license (remotion.pro). gstack doesn't bundle Remotion, the
project installs it from npm.

## Step 1 — Pin the brief

From the user's request, decide and state in one short block:

- **Format:** width × height and fps. Defaults: landscape `1920x1080@30`.
  Vertical / Reels / TikTok / Shorts → `1080x1920@30`. Square → `1080x1080@30`.
- **Duration:** seconds → `durationInFrames = seconds × fps`. Default 10s if
  the user gave no hint. Over 60s: confirm with AskUserQuestion first, render
  time grows linearly.
- **Scenes:** a numbered list, each with its frame range and what moves.
- **Assets:** any images, audio, video, or fonts the user referenced. Local
  files get copied into `<projdir>/public/` and loaded with `staticFile()`.
- **Codec:** `h264` MP4 by default. `vp8` for WebM, `gif` for GIF, `prores`
  only when the user mentions editing in Premiere/Final Cut.

If the request is too vague to pick scenes (e.g. "make a video" with no
subject), ask ONE AskUserQuestion: what the video is about and where it will
be posted. Everything else has a default.

Decide `<projdir>`: `./videos/<slug>/` when the cwd is a git repo (the user can
commit the source), else `/tmp/gstack-videos/<slug>/`. `<slug>` is kebab-case,
≤40 chars, from the video's subject. If `<projdir>` already holds a Remotion
project (`package.json` lists `remotion`), reuse it: skip to Step 3 and edit.

## Step 2 — Scaffold the project (once per project)

Never run `npx create-video`: it is interactive and pulls a template you'd
throw away. Write the files directly.

Resolve ONE Remotion version and use it for every `remotion` / `@remotion/*`
package. Mismatched versions are the #1 Remotion breakage ("Version mismatch"
at render time):

```bash
npm view remotion version 2>/dev/null | tail -1
```

Remember that version as `<rv>`. If the lookup fails (offline registry), stop
and tell the user npm is unreachable. Don't guess a version.

Write these files with the Write tool:

`<projdir>/package.json`:

```json
{
  "name": "<slug>",
  "private": true,
  "type": "module",
  "scripts": {
    "studio": "remotion studio src/index.ts",
    "render": "remotion render src/index.ts Main out/<slug>.mp4"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "@remotion/cli": "<rv>",
    "remotion": "<rv>",
    "react": "19.1.0",
    "react-dom": "19.1.0"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "@types/react": "19.1.8",
    "typescript": "5.8.3"
  }
}
```

Add `@remotion/google-fonts` (same `<rv>`) only if the video uses a Google
font, `@remotion/transitions` (same `<rv>`) only for scene transitions.

`<projdir>/tsconfig.json`:

```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ES2022",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "strict": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "noEmit": true
  },
  "include": ["src"]
}
```

`<projdir>/.gitignore`:

```
node_modules/
out/
```

`<projdir>/src/index.ts`:

```ts
import { registerRoot } from 'remotion';
import { RemotionRoot } from './Root';

registerRoot(RemotionRoot);
```

`<projdir>/src/Root.tsx`: one `<Composition id="Main" ...>` with the format and
duration from Step 1, plus `defaultProps` for any text the user may want to
change later (title, tagline, CTA). Props make re-renders a one-flag change.

Then install:

```bash
cd <projdir> && npm install --no-audit --no-fund 2>&1 | tail -5
```

## Step 3 — Author the composition

Write `<projdir>/src/Main.tsx` (split scenes into `src/scenes/*.tsx` when there
are more than 3). Remotion rules that are not optional, every one of them
breaks rendering or makes frames non-deterministic:

- **All motion comes from `useCurrentFrame()`.** Drive every change with
  `interpolate(frame, [in, out], [from, to], { extrapolateLeft: 'clamp', extrapolateRight: 'clamp' })`
  or `spring({ frame, fps, config: { damping: 200 } })`. Never use CSS
  `transition`, CSS `@keyframes`, `setTimeout`, `requestAnimationFrame`, or
  animation libraries that run on wall-clock time. Each frame is rendered
  independently, wall-clock animation renders as a frozen or flickering mess.
- **No `Math.random()`.** Use `random('seed-string')` from `remotion`, it is
  stable across frames and render workers.
- **Timeline with `<Sequence from={..} durationInFrames={..}>`.** Inside a
  Sequence, `useCurrentFrame()` restarts at 0, write each scene as if it
  starts at frame 0.
- **Layout with `<AbsoluteFill>`** for full-frame layers. Size text in px
  relative to `useVideoConfig().width`, so the same scene works vertical and
  landscape.
- **Media via Remotion components:** `<Img src={staticFile('logo.png')} />`,
  `<OffthreadVideo>`, `<Audio>`. A plain `<img>` can render before it loads and
  produce blank frames. Remote URLs work but make the render network-dependent;
  prefer copying assets into `public/`.
- **Fonts:** `loadFont()` from `@remotion/google-fonts/<Family>`, or
  `@font-face` on a file in `public/`. System fonts differ between machines.
- Keep text on screen long enough to read: ~3 words per second, minimum 1.5s
  per text block, plus a 0.3-0.5s ease in and out.

Typecheck before rendering, it's seconds and catches most mistakes:

```bash
cd <projdir> && npx tsc -p . 2>&1 | tail -20
```

## Step 4 — Check stills, then render

Render one still per scene (the middle frame of each scene's range) and look
at them BEFORE the full render. A bad layout costs seconds here and minutes in
a full render:

```bash
cd <projdir> && npx remotion still src/index.ts Main out/check-<frame>.png --frame=<frame> 2>&1 | tail -3
```

First run downloads Chrome Headless Shell (~100MB, once per machine). If that
download fails (`getaddrinfo` / `remotion.media` errors), reuse the Chromium
that Playwright already installed for gstack's browse daemon:

```bash
for d in "${PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH:-}" "$HOME/.cache/ms-playwright" "$HOME/Library/Caches/ms-playwright"; do
  [ -n "$d" ] && [ -d "$d" ] && find "$d" -maxdepth 4 -type f \( -name headless_shell -o -name chrome-headless-shell \) 2>/dev/null
done | head -1
```

Pass the printed path as `--browser-executable=<path>` on every `still` /
`render` call (remember it for the session). If it fails with
missing shared libraries on Linux, show the error and the line from
https://www.remotion.dev/docs/miscellaneous/linux-dependencies, then stop.

Read every still with the Read tool. Check: text not clipped at the frame
edges, nothing overlapping by accident, contrast readable, assets actually
loaded (no blank boxes). Fix and re-check until they're right.

Then the full render:

```bash
cd <projdir> && npx remotion render src/index.ts Main out/<slug>.mp4 --codec=h264 2>&1 | tail -5
```

Swap `--codec` and the extension per Step 1 (`vp8` → `.webm`, `gif` → `.gif`,
`prores` → `.mov`). To override text without editing code:
`--props='{"title":"New title"}'`.

Verify the output exists and is the length you planned:

```bash
ls -lh <projdir>/out/<slug>.*
command -v ffprobe >/dev/null && ffprobe -v error -show_entries format=duration -of csv=p=0 <projdir>/out/<slug>.mp4
```

If the render fails, read the error, fix the source, re-run Step 4. Don't hand
the user a project that hasn't rendered.

## Step 5 — Deliver

1. Show 1-3 of the checked stills inline (Read tool) so the user sees the video
   without opening it.
2. Give the video path, duration, resolution, and file size.
3. Give the two commands they'll want next:
   - `cd <projdir> && npm run studio` opens Remotion Studio, a live preview
     with a timeline and editable props in the browser.
   - `cd <projdir> && npm run render` re-renders after edits.
4. If the user wants changes: edit the TSX (or pass `--props`), re-check the
   affected stills, re-render. The source is the single source of truth.

## Rules

- **Never deliver a video you haven't rendered and looked at.** If rendering
  is impossible (Node missing, npm or the browser download blocked), say so,
  deliver the project source, and stop.
- **One Remotion version per project.** Adding a `@remotion/*` package later?
  Pin it to the version already in `package.json`.
- **Don't commit `out/` or `node_modules/`.** The scaffolded `.gitignore`
  handles it; mention that the source in `src/` is what belongs in git.
- For a diagram inside a video: render it with `/diagram` first and use the
  SVG/PNG as an asset in `public/`.

## Completion status

- DONE — video rendered, stills shown, project path and re-render commands given.
- DONE_WITH_CONCERNS — rendered, but something was degraded (remote assets,
  substituted font, duration trimmed). Say what.
- BLOCKED — Node/npm or the headless browser unavailable; the exact blocker
  and fix surfaced.
